{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Any man, to any close flesh-relative, you shall not approach, etc.</b> The 'approaching' refers to that which leads to uncovering of nakedness — and that is seclusion (yichud), for it is what leads to nakedness. And therefore it says 'any man' (אִישׁ אִישׁ) — specifically when each and every man is alone with the woman, for two men are permitted to be secluded with one woman; and 'אִישׁ אִישׁ' means nothing other than every man alone. And it says 'you shall not approach' (לֹא תִקְרְבוּ) in plural language, to warn the female as well, as Rashi explained. And the reason for the prohibition of forbidden relations — the Rambam explained that it is to diminish promiscuity, for these women are close to him and constantly available to him. And the Ramban overturned this view, for we have seen that Scripture permitted him women by the thousands. Therefore he explained that this commandment is a secret (סוֹד). And it appears that for this reason 'אֲנִי ה׳' is stated concerning it — that is to say: I am the One who knows the reason for the matter, and for you it is a statute (חֻקָּה)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:14.409885464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}